@meta
id: 10-01
title: Change the word you are standing on
chapter: 10
type: lesson
skills: obj-word, inner-vs-around
par: 46
minutes: 5
vscode: double-click the word, type the new one, repeat
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Everything so far has been operator plus motion: the operator acts on the
ground between where the cursor is and where the motion lands. That works,
but it makes you responsible for standing in the right place first. `cw`
from the middle of a word changes the second half of it.

A text object is the other half of the grammar. It is not a direction, it
is a thing: the word, the string, the argument list, the block. You say
which thing you mean and vim works out where it starts and ends. The
cursor only has to be somewhere inside it.

Someone has reviewed this provider and asked for one change: the local
variable called `record` reads like a database row. It is an order. Rename
all three of them.

Nothing else in the file changes.

@keys
| `iw` | inner word — the word the cursor is inside |
| `ciw` | change that word, from anywhere in it |
| `diw` | delete it |

@hints
`cw` only works if you are on the first letter. You have a better option
now — one that does not care where in the word you landed.
--
`ciw` changes the whole word under the cursor and drops you into insert
mode with it gone.
--
`f` gets you onto the word, `ciw` replaces it, `j` or a count of `j`
carries you to the next one. You never need to aim at the first letter.

@solution
    11j fr ciw order <Esc>
    2j ciw order <Esc>
    4j ciw order <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Read the second and third edits again. After the first `ciw` the cursor
sits in the middle of `order`; `2j` carries that column down to the middle
of the next `record`, and `ciw` still does exactly the right thing. No
`b`, no `^`, no aiming.

That is the whole argument for text objects. Operator plus motion asks
where do I want to go. Operator plus object asks what do I mean. The
second question is nearly always easier to answer, and it is the one your
hands can answer without looking.

`iw` is precise about what a word is: letters, digits and underscores.
Punctuation is a word of its own. In `order.total` the cursor on `total`
gives you `total`, not the whole expression. That is usually what you
wanted, and when it is not there is another object for it.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const record = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!record) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return record;
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }
}
